[ti:American Culture: Bald Eagle Officially Named National Bird of U.S.]
[by:www.21voa.com]
[00:00.00]更多听力请访问21VOA.COM
[00:00.04]The bald eagle is a symbol of the United States.
[00:04.76]An image of the bird is on the flag
[00:07.84]and seal of the U.S. president,
[00:10.64]the seal of the U.S. Supreme Court,
[00:14.16]military insignia, as well as the dollar bill.
[00:19.92]Even so, the bird has had its ups and downs.
[00:25.96]At one time, the bald eagle was at risk
[00:29.00]of permanent disappearance, or extinction.
[00:33.88]Later, however, its numbers recovered.
[00:37.24]In the early 2000s, the federal government
[00:41.08]removed the bird from its endangered species list.
[00:46.36]Culturally, too, the raptors are doing well again.
[00:51.00]In December 2024 before leaving office,
[00:55.64]Joe Biden signed legislation
[00:58.96]officially making the bald eagle
[01:01.40]the national bird of the United States
[01:05.92]The bald eagle has a long complex story
[01:09.40]of species weakness and recovery.
[01:12.96]An insect-killing chemical called DDT
[01:17.52]played a major part in that story.
[01:21.92]DDT is a chemical used to kill insects.
[01:27.12]The substance also proved harmful to eagle eggs.
[01:32.40]American officials banned DDT in 1972.
[01:38.60]But the use of the chemical had already caused
[01:42.76]severe damage to eagle populations.
[01:47.40]For example, in the 1980s
[01:50.24]there was only one nesting pair of eagles
[01:54.00]in the state of New Jersey.
[01:57.20]Now, there are about 300 pairs.
[02:01.28]To rebuild the number of eagles across the U.S.,
[02:06.16]researchers wanting to save them
[02:08.76]imported the birds from places
[02:11.36]where their population numbers
[02:13.32]were secure, including Canada.
[02:17.92]Early on, they also removed eggs from their nests
[02:22.28]so they could safely hatch with scientists
[02:26.16]before being returned to their parents to raise,
[02:30.08]explained Kathy Clark.
[02:33.20]She is the head of New Jersey's Endangered
[02:36.96]and Nongame Species Program.
[02:40.52]Clark has worked with the birds for many years.
[02:44.60]This includes the time
[02:46.56]when New Jersey had very few bald eagles,
[02:49.80]and they only lived in areas far from people.
[02:56.04]Clark said that she will always remember that feeling
[02:59.72]of not knowing if the species will survive.
[03:04.24]Maia Edwards is the science director
[03:07.84]at the American Bald Eagle Foundation in Alaska.
[03:12.12]She says the species represents
[03:15.32]"one of the few conservation success stories of animals"
[03:19.88]that came close to disappearing
[03:22.12]permanently from America.
[03:25.40]"...I think now it's really important
[03:28.52]to make sure people know that story
[03:31.36]and learn from it," she said.
[03:35.36]Clark said the eagles have taught officials some lessons.
[03:39.88]One is that the raptors
[03:42.48]are good at changing to fit their environment.
[03:46.68]The birds now live in parts of New Jersey
[03:49.76]that are dense with people.
[03:52.56]But, the birds still face dangers in such areas.
[03:57.56]Jilian Fazio is director of
[04:00.48]the Essex County Turtle Back Zoo
[04:02.92]in northern New Jersey.
[04:05.16]She said the zoo rescues a number of birds hit by cars.
[04:11.32]The zoo also is home to Freedom,
[04:14.36]a bald eagle named by the caretakers.
[04:18.68]Freedom was rescued
[04:20.48]hanging from a tree in Minnesota
[04:22.96]after he got caught in some rope.
[04:26.84]He was injured so badly
[04:28.80]that he cannot be returned to the wild, Fazio said.
[04:34.04]A lack of eagle habitat and prey
[04:37.96]also threatens the species.
[04:41.52]Bald eagles feed heavily on fish,
[04:45.20]so they require clean, open water in which to hunt.
[04:50.84]Disease outbreaks, such as avian influenza or bird flu,
[04:56.44]are another serious threat to the birds.
[05:01.20]The birds are highly respected
[05:03.52]by several Native American cultures
[05:06.44]as symbols of strength,
[05:09.12]especially in the Pacific Northwest, says Paul Apodaca.
[05:15.72]He is a retired university professor
[05:18.80]who specializes in stories
[05:21.40]and storytelling from different cultures.
[05:26.20]The position of eagles as symbols of the U.S.
[05:29.72]adds to their protection.
[05:32.64]"Americans are always going to have
[05:35.24]that sort of personal relationship
[05:38.00]with bald eagles," said Clark,
[05:40.68]the New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection official.
[05:45.84]Dan Day is a birder.
[05:48.16]He has seen bald eagles
[05:50.40]about 50 times in recent years
[05:53.80]both on nature walks and while driving around.
[05:59.20]He said, "It really elevates your day
[06:02.20]to see a bald eagle."
[06:04.88]I'm Anna Matteo. 更多听力请访问21VOA.COM